[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue, "dates parse mm/dd in eu locales", and marked
known. Read by cause instead of by subject line, its reports hold separate problems. Each one has
its own fix, so each one now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October with the parser fixed and the booking-v3 form
retired. The <code>one-day</code> moves had no fix in that update: their ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} {status} />

## What each one is

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants write the day first,
  so day and month swap: a booking for 7 April arrives as 4 July. This is the bug #4471 names.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants
  type what the hint says, and the form reads it day first. Same symptom, different code: the fix
  is the hint, not the parser. Only reports sent through that form at booking-v3 tenants.
- **`one-day`**: the desk read these reports as a one-day move, not a swap, and listed them in
  `desk-notes.json`: "Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May", "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday.
  Client in Toronto got Monday.", "Every booking in June is one day off." No day/month swap does
  that. Our best guess is time zones; one report ("the invite arrived before I made the booking")
  fits that only loosely. The desk counts these reports under `parser` too, where it files them
  by tenant; until now they had no ticket of their own, only a footnote.

## Why one ticket hid them

- **The merge rule.** [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject
  line. Subjects name the symptom, not the cause: "Date is wrong" and "Month and day swapped?" turn
  up under every problem above.
- **The intake tag.** `meta.intakeTag` is a guess from the reporter's browser: every en-US browser
  is tagged `hint`, every other one `parser`. The cause depends on the tenant, its booking form and
  the channel (`causes.ts`), so the tag often disagrees with the problem (see "tagged as" above).
  Don't count by it.
- **The page said one thing, its table another.** The incident page's own table showed more than
  one cause, and a footnote for the one-day moves, under a callout that said "one issue".

## What changes

- Track each problem in its own ticket (linked in the table) and close each with its own fix:
  `parser` is closed as fixed, `hint` as won't fix (the form is gone), `one-day` stays open until
  someone owns it.
- Merge by cause (tenant, booking form, channel), not by subject. Decision 0001 stays as written;
  it needs a new record to replace it.
- [Triage: duplicate reports](/kb/triage/dedupe) still teaches the subject rule and is stale.
  Re-read it with the new record.

